Henry J Cobb <hcobb@io.com> writes:
> So did the black light light up a corridor space?

Yes. Black lights are a small-radius light source. (As are yellow
lights, fire vortices and elementals, and flaming and shocking
spheres.) It was probably round a corner, if you only saw one square
of its light.

And yes, you can see the reflected light, but not the black light
itself.  An old explanation of mine: the magic of invisibility edits
out your brain's impression of the light cast by the y itself, but
doesn't alter the light being reflected off the surroundings, allowing
it to be a light source. (In much the same way that the Ring made
Bilbo invisible, but not his shadow.)

